<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sher Shah Suri</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1486–<a href="/years/1545">1545</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sher Shah Suri was an Afghan ruler who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sur-empire">Sur Empire</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. After defeating the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a> at Chausa in 1539 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a> in 1540, he ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> until he was killed at the siege of Kalinjar in 1545. His land revenue system, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupiya">rupiya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-trunk-road">Grand Trunk Road</a> with its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a> were taken over by the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri.jpg" alt="Sher Shah Suri" width="320" height="473" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Court painters of Mughal emperor Akbar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Painting_of_Sher_Shah_Suri_(colored)_from_a_manuscript_of_Tarikh-i-Khandan-i-Timuriya,_prepared_by_the_court_painters_of_Mughal_emperor_Akbar,_circa_16th_century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sher Shah was born Farid Khan in Sasaram in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, a Pashtun of the Sur clan. His birth year is disputed, with some accounts giving 1472 and others 1486. His grandfather Ibrahim Khan Sur migrated from the region of Roh in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and began as a horse trader, and his father Hasan Khan Sur held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagirs</a> in Bihar. He received the title Sher Khan, from Persian for lion, after killing a tiger that leapt at the governor of Bihar.</p><p>He managed his father's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagirs</a> for 21 years, reforming land revenue assessment and the commissions of tax collectors, until the resentment of his stepmother and step-brothers led him to resign in 1518. He went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>, observed the Mughal leadership of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> and then entered the service of the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>. When Sultan Mohammad of Bihar died in October 1528, the queen Dudu Bibi became regent and Sher Shah her deputy governor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>By 1530 Sher Shah was regent and de facto ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>. He gained the fort of Chunar by marrying the widow of its governor, defeated the Lohani chiefs of Bihar and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> at Surajgarh in March 1534, and in 1538 took Rohtasgarh and Gauda while the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a> was engaged elsewhere. After defeating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. He then conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> in 1542, and in 1543 took Raisen, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajputs</a> who left the fort under a promise of safe conduct were massacred, an act considered the darkest mark on his reign.</p><p>He ordered the empire's administration and army. He divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> into 47 administrative divisions, paid his soldiers fixed salaries, assigned roles through the Dagh system and promoted by merit. At provincial borders he abolished taxes, leaving only a levy on goods brought in and one on goods sold. From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittagong">Chittagong</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> he extended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-trunk-road">Grand Trunk Road</a> and issued a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coin of 178 grains called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupee</a>, the precursor of the modern rupee.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sher Shah besieged Kalinjar fort for seven months, and the circumstances of his death are uncertain. In one account a bomb thrown at the fort fell back on a store of bombs, and he was found half-burned and lived two more days, ordering the assault until he heard the fort had fallen. He died on 22 May 1545. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam-shah-suri">Jalal Khan</a>, known as Islam Shah Suri, won the succession and ruled until 1553, after which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sur-empire">Sur Empire</a> fell apart in civil war until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> reconquered it. Historians debate his religious policy, and many hold that he showed tolerance toward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sher Shah Suri” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…in the north brought harder enemies: Babur defeated a Bengal army at the Battle of the Ghaghra in 1529, and <b>Sher Khan Sur</b> defeated the sultan's forces at Surajgarh in 1534 and twice besieged Gaur, taking it in 1538 despite Portuguese help to the last Husain Shahi sultan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The early Mughal state nonetheless remained precarious; his son Humayun was driven into exile by <b>Sher Shah Suri</b> before recovering Delhi in 1555.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Afghan forces under <b>Sher Shah Suri</b>, aided by the Ujjainiya Rajputs of Bhojpur, defeated the Mughal emperor Humayun at Chausa, southwest of Buxar in modern-day Bihar.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-26">26 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Rao Maldeo (r. 1532–1562) made Marwar a regional power before <b>Sher Shah</b>'s Sur Empire defeated his army at Sammel in 1544.</blockquote><a href="/state/jodhpur-state">Jodhpur State</a></li><li><blockquote>The Gujarat Sultanate conquered Malwa in 1531, and <b>Sher Shah Suri</b> later took it.</blockquote><a href="/state/malwa-sultanate">Malwa Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Farid Khan</b>, an Afghan noble of the Sur clan in Bihar, earned the title Sher Khan and gathered power in eastern India.</blockquote><a href="/state/sur-empire">Sur Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sasaram, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinjar-fort">Kalinjar Fort</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=24.95,84.03&amp;year=1524" aria-label="Sasaram on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6779 1246 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sher Shah Suri"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7229 1516h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7229 1516h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=24.95,84.03&amp;year=1524">What was here in 1524? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
